cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "FlyLab: typed pharmacological evidence and connectome-dependence testing on the Drosophila MaleCNS connectome"
message: >-
  Cite the connectome papers first (MaleCNS v1.0, FlyWire), then this software.
  Nothing in FlyLab is a measurement from a living animal.
type: software
authors:
  - name: "FlyLab contributors"
version: 0.6.0
date-released: "2026-09-19"
license: MIT
repository-code: https://github.com/pinkysworld/FlyLab
url: https://github.com/pinkysworld/FlyLab
abstract: >-
  FlyLab maps a compound and a free concentration onto a typed engagement
  panel for insect receptors and their vertebrate counterparts at the same
  dose, converts the insect side through a fixed mechanism table into gain
  multipliers on transmitter classes of a named MaleCNS v1.0 connectome cut,
  runs a deterministic rate model or a Shiu-style leaky integrate-and-fire
  model over that cut, and exports one notebook JSON with a provenance block
  (version, git SHA, compound-library SHA-256, map identifiers, seeds). The
  evidence is typed: the parameter a source measured and its distance from the
  modelled target decide whether FlyLab may compute a binding occupancy, a
  functional engagement, or nothing at all, so unsupported receptors return
  N/A rather than a small number. Around that it ships the analyses that test
  its own predictions: connectome-dependence analysis (empirical permutation
  probabilities against degraded graph models, with a three-way verdict
  against a prespecified equivalence margin and the weakest graph model the
  test could not distinguish from the real cut), an ablation ladder over the
  pipeline's layers, a conclusion-stability matrix over a prespecified family
  of gain specifications, a variance-based uncertainty budget with a
  value-of-information ranking, a receptor-versus-circuit selectivity
  landscape, literature concordance against published orderings, published
  resistance alleles as per-compound potency shifts, combination-effect null
  models and a set of prospective predictions. It is not a new connectome, not
  regulatory toxicology and not a source of live-animal data.

keywords:
  - connectomics
  - Drosophila melanogaster
  - in-silico pharmacology
  - evidence typing
  - receptor engagement
  - insecticide selectivity
  - null models
  - sensitivity analysis
  - specification robustness
  - reproducible research
  - 3Rs
identifiers:
  - type: other
    value: "Zenodo DOI to be minted on release"
    description: Archived release DOI (placeholder until the deposit exists)
references:
  - title: MaleCNS v1.0 whole adult male Drosophila CNS connectome
    type: article
    year: 2026
    notes: >-
      HHMI Janelia FlyEM, University of Cambridge, MRC LMB and Google Research.
      CC-BY 4.0. The netlist FlyLab patches; downloaded, never redistributed.
  - title: "Neuronal wiring diagram of an adult brain (FlyWire)"
    type: article
    year: 2024
    doi: 10.1038/s41586-024-07558-y
  - title: >-
      A leaky integrate-and-fire computational model based on the connectome of
      the entire adult Drosophila brain reveals insights into sensorimotor
      processing
    type: article
    year: 2024
    notes: Shiu, Sterne, Spiller et al., Nature 634. Source of the LIF parameters and the bitter-veto direction used as a control.
  - title: >-
      Accelerating with FlyBrainLab the discovery of the functional logic of
      the Drosophila brain in the connectomic and synaptomic era
    type: article
    year: 2021
    doi: 10.7554/eLife.62362
    notes: >-
      Lazar, Liu, Turkcan, Zhou, eLife 10:e62362. Executable fly circuits built
      from connectome data already exist; FlyLab is positioned against this,
      not as the first such tool.
  - title: >-
      Whole brain modelling for simulating pharmacological interventions on
      patients with disorders of consciousness
    type: article
    year: 2024
    doi: 10.1038/s42003-024-06852-9
    notes: >-
      Mindlin et al., Commun. Biol. 7:1176. Receptor-density-weighted
      perturbation of a human whole-brain model, which likewise found the
      effect dominated by overall receptor presence rather than its placement.
